<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Capital Cities and Tombs of the Ancient Koguryo Kingdom</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Capital Cities and Tombs of the Ancient Koguryo Kingdom is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, inscribed in 2004. It covers the mountain fortresses of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wunu-mountain">Wunü Mountain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hwando">Hwando</a>, the walled city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gungnae">Gungnae</a> and some forty royal and noble tombs around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huanren">Huanren</a> and Ji'an, together with the stele of King Gwanggaeto. The sites document the early centuries of the Korean kingdom of Goguryeo.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/capital-cities-and-tombs-of-the-ancient-koguryo-kingdom.jpg" alt="Capital Cities and Tombs of the Ancient Koguryo Kingdom" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: EditQ · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tomb_of_King_Jangsu_4.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
